WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.070 --> 00:00:04.910
So in the early days of this disease the first conservation management action

00:00:04.910 --> 00:00:09.769
was to put some devils away somewhere
safe, isolated from the disease

00:00:09.769 --> 00:00:13.809
where we could maintain a population of 
healthy devils

00:00:13.809 --> 00:00:16.920
that could be used to reintroduce them to
the wild

00:00:16.920 --> 00:00:20.710
The rate at which Tasmanian devils have been declining throughout Tasmania

00:00:20.710 --> 00:00:24.130
makes the threat of extinction a possible
outcome

00:00:24.130 --> 00:00:27.500
and we're quite concerned about that and
that's where we're trying to put our efforts

00:00:27.500 --> 00:00:29.779
and to learn more about the disease
itself.

00:00:29.779 --> 00:00:34.400
At the moment we're working with the
genome sequence and with a large drug screen

00:00:34.500 --> 00:00:36.200
here at the Sanger Institute

00:00:36.200 --> 00:00:39.600
to try to identify targeted molecules which act against

00:00:39.600 --> 00:00:43.000
specific mutated proteins in the devil cancer 

00:00:43.000 --> 00:00:45.600
which may be effective treatments for this disease.

00:00:46.000 --> 00:00:49.500
We still have a lot of work to do but
we have researchers from

00:00:49.500 --> 00:00:55.700
all over the world working on this
problem, in the UK and in Tasmania, in Hobart

00:00:55.900 --> 00:01:00.300
and so, we hope that eventually we will have
something that could tip the balance

00:01:00.400 --> 00:01:02.400
in favour of the Tasmanian devil

00:01:02.400 --> 00:01:05.000
and give them a fighting chance to survive in the wild.

